WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:10.576
If you've ever watched a path traced preview, in Blender or a modern game, you've seen it. A grainy image, sprinkled with bright white specks, slowly settling down.

00:00:10.926 --> 00:00:20.547
Here's a lacquered sphere in a dark gallery, lit by one small spotlight. Look at the felt wall behind it. It sparkles, even though it's plain matte cloth.

00:00:20.897 --> 00:00:32.417
Here's the odd part. With the same number of rays, one way of aiming them leaves bright speckles on the shiny sphere. Another way cleans up the sphere, but leaves dark holes everywhere else.

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:37.290
Same scene, same rays. Only the aim changes. So why the difference?

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:46.981
For every pixel, a renderer asks how much light bounces toward the camera. That means adding up light from every direction above the surface.

00:00:47.331 --> 00:00:53.731
It can't check every direction, so it picks a few at random and averages them. That's called Monte Carlo.

00:00:54.081 --> 00:01:01.953
Each random direction adds the material's response, times the incoming light, times a cosine, all divided by p.

00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:13.183
And p matters. It's the probability of picking that direction. Directions we pick often get divided by a big number, rare picks by a small one, so the average stays fair.

00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:20.359
So we can aim the rays any way we like, and the answer is still right on average. What changes is the noise.

00:01:21.559 --> 00:01:25.869
We'll compare two ways to aim. Think of them as two kinds of lottery ticket.

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:34.283
The cosine ticket spreads rays over the whole sky above the surface, favouring straight up. It's a perfect match for matte surfaces.

00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:45.491
The Phong ticket packs rays into a narrow cone around the mirror direction, where a shiny highlight lives. How tight the cone is depends on an exponent, here thirty-two.

00:01:45.841 --> 00:01:54.225
Here are the two real densities, measured over the sky. The cosine one is broad and gentle. The Phong one is a single hot spot.

00:01:55.425 --> 00:02:00.503
Now add the spotlight. Seen from the sphere, it's tiny, just a few degrees across.

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:09.663
With the cosine ticket, almost every ray misses it. But once in a while a ray hits that very bright light, and p for that direction is small.

00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:18.269
Bright light divided by a small probability makes one enormous sample. That single pixel blows out to white. That's a firefly.

00:02:18.619 --> 00:02:23.654
The cosine ticket produced over ten times as many fireflies as the Phong ticket.

00:02:24.004 --> 00:02:28.612
And the error on the highlight drops to about a quarter, for the same number of rays.

00:02:29.812 --> 00:02:34.868
This is the measured error map of the highlight, with both tickets on the same colour scale.

00:02:35.218 --> 00:02:41.213
Under the cosine ticket, a hot blob sits right on the highlight, spiked with red fireflies.

00:02:41.563 --> 00:02:46.896
Under the Phong ticket, the highlight cools down. The error doesn't vanish, though. It moves.

00:02:48.096 --> 00:02:52.128
So where did it go? Look at the dull plinth and the matte side of the sphere.

00:02:52.478 --> 00:03:01.630
The Phong ticket sends nearly all its rays toward the mirror direction. Matte surfaces need light from the whole sky, and hardly any rays go there.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:07.527
Their error jumps nearly six times higher under Phong. They starve, and dark holes appear.

00:03:07.877 --> 00:03:19.525
Noise isn't bad luck. It's the mismatch between where you aim and where the light really comes from. Each ticket wins exactly where its shape matches the material, and loses everywhere else.

00:03:20.725 --> 00:03:28.405
More rays help, but slowly. Under the cosine ticket, the highlight error halves each time the ray count goes up four times.

00:03:28.755 --> 00:03:35.432
Four times the rays, half the error. Phong walks down the same staircase, but starts about four times lower.

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:47.195
The exponent matters too. Over a middle range the highlight error stays low. Push the cone much narrower and the error climbs right back up. Aim too tight, and you miss the light again.

00:03:48.395 --> 00:03:58.934
A few limits. It's one sphere, one small light and one direct bounce, rendered offline on a software rasterizer. Not real time, and not hardware ray tracing.

00:03:59.284 --> 00:04:09.972
One surprise we kept. In a plain white test room, the combined Phong estimate came out about a third too low. Its far tail was lost to floating point precision.

00:04:10.322 --> 00:04:18.023
The cosine estimate landed right on the expected value. Every check in the run passed, and here's the clean reference image.

00:04:19.223 --> 00:04:23.853
Every way of aiming rays is fair on average. The noise comes from mismatch.

00:04:24.203 --> 00:04:30.176
Match the cosine for matte surfaces. Match the lobe for shiny ones. Neither one matches both.

00:04:30.526 --> 00:04:38.995
That's why production path tracers blend several strategies with multiple importance sampling, which an earlier video in this series covers.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:48.135
For further reading, there's Lafortune and Willems on the normalized Phong model, and Eric Veach's thesis on robust Monte Carlo rendering.

